Unlock LiteLLM's MIT-licensed JWT auth (litellm/proxy/auth/handle_jwt.py,
outside enterprise/) so an OIDC access token acts as its Internal User.

- Remove the premium_user gate in front of `general_settings.enable_jwt_auth`.
- Require the RFC 9068 section 4 `typ: at+jwt` header so ID tokens and other
  JWTs are never accepted as access tokens. Upstream JWT auth serves any IdP
  JWT, including Keycloak's default `typ: JWT`, so this is this image's token
  profile rather than an upstream bug.
- Carry a teamless JWT user's models onto the token, as upstream already does
  for UI session tokens. Invocation enforces them through common_checks, but
  /v1/models, /model/info, and the key-level checks (client fallbacks,
  Responses WebSocket first frame, access-group routing) read the token's
  models, so a restricted user would otherwise see and reach every model.
- Skip the single-team fallback. Upstream attaches a user's only team to a
  teamless token, which replaces the user's models and personal budget with
  the team's; adding a user to a team for MCP grants must not change that.
- On MCP routes, admit the user as LiteLLM admits its own MCP logins
  (MCPRequestHandler.reload_admitted_user), so they reach the servers granted
  to them and to each of their teams, or an admin's view. Managed agent
  identities keep upstream's delegated MCP authorization.

Checks: tests/verify_jwt_auth.py

